#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# discrete_limit

#% text
# `discrete_limit(f, n -> inf)` gives the limit of the sequence `f(n)` as the integer `n` tends to infinity.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `fibonacci` and `lucas_l` are written by Binet's formulas and powers `(-c)^n` as `(-1)^n c^n`.

#% item
# A sequence with `(-1)^n` is split into its even and odd terms: when their limits agree that is the limit, otherwise the result is `failure(no_limit, discrete_limit, f)`.

#% item
# Factorials, binomials and other gamma quotients are taken through Stirling's series.

#% item
# A list of rules `[m -> inf, n -> inf]` takes the limits one after the other, the last rule first; a list `f` maps elementwise.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
discrete_limit(n/(n + 1), n -> inf)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# An alternating sequence that tends to 0:

#% cell
discrete_limit((-5)^n/factorial(n), n -> inf)
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Ratios of Fibonacci and Lucas numbers:

#% cell
[discrete_limit(fibonacci(n)/lucas_l(n), n -> inf), discrete_limit(fibonacci(n)/2^n, n -> inf)]
#% output
#= [(1/5)*5^(1/2), 0]

#% text
# Factorials against exponentials:

#% cell
discrete_limit(factorial(n)/3^n/exp(n), n -> inf)
#% output
#= inf

#% text
# Several sequences at once:

#% cell
discrete_limit([n/(n + 2), exp(-n/(n + 1))], n -> inf)
#% output
#= [1, exp(-1)]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The even and odd terms of `(-1)^n` have different limits:

#% cell
discrete_limit((-1)^n, n -> inf)
#% output
#! failure(no_limit, discrete_limit, (-1)^n)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `limit`, `sum`, `fibonacci`
